    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Not necessarily. The same zeroes can give equal or different discriminants.\n\nFor $g(x)=\\lambda f(x)$ with $\\lambda\\ne0$, $\\Delta_g=\\lambda^2\\Delta_f$.",
      "worked": "The quadratics $f(x)=x^2-x-2$ and $g(x)=2x^2-2x-4$ both have zeroes $-1$ and $2$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta_f&=(-1)^2-4(1)(-2)\\\\\\Delta_f&=9\\\\\\Delta_g&=(-2)^2-4(2)(-4)\\\\\\Delta_g&=36\\end{align*}$$\n\nTheir zeroes are the same, but their discriminants differ. Thus equal zeroes do not guarantee equal discriminants.\n\nIf $f(x)=ax^2+bx+c$ and $g(x)=\\lambda f(x)$, where $\\lambda\\ne0$, then\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta_g&=(\\lambda b)^2-4(\\lambda a)(\\lambda c)\\\\\\Delta_g&=\\lambda^2(b^2-4ac)\\\\\\Delta_g&=\\lambda^2\\Delta_f\\end{align*}$$\n\nEqual discriminants are still possible: $f$ and $-f$ have the same discriminant. Scalar multiples of a quadratic with a repeated root both have discriminant $0$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 20
